      "answer": "Perfect! I now have a complete understanding. Let me provide a comprehensive answer to the user:\n\n## Prompt Cache Breakpoint Location for Claude Requests\n\nBased on the codebase, there are **two places** where prompt cache breakpoints are placed for Claude requests:\n\n### 1. **Trailing Breakpoint on the Last Message Block** (Lines 198–206 in `convert.ts`)\n```typescript\n// Trailing prompt-cache breakpoint over the conversation prefix, so the\n// stable history caches across turns. Skipped when the caller opts out.\nif (cacheRetention !== \"none\") {\n  const lastMessage = merged[merged.length - 1]!;\n  const lastBlock = lastMessage.content[lastMessage.content.length - 1];\n  if (lastBlock) {\n    lastBlock.cache_control = { type: \"ephemeral\" };\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis places `cache_control: { type: \"ephemeral\" }` on the **last content block** of the final message in the conversation. This ensures the entire conversation history up to that point is cached for subsequent requests.\n\n### 2. **Breakpoint on the Last Tool Definition** (Lines 254–258 in `convert.ts`)\n```typescript\n// Cache the (static) tool definitions with a breakpoint on the last\n// tool. Skipped when the caller opts out of caching.\n...(cacheRetention !== \"none\" \u0026\u0026 index === tools.length - 1\n  ? { cache_control: { type: \"ephemeral\" } }\n  : {}),\n```\n\nThis places `cache_control: { type: \"ephemeral\" }` on the **last tool** in the tools array to cache all tool definitions as a single unit.\n\n### Key Notes:\n- Both breakpoints are **only added when `cacheRetention !== \"none\"`** — callers can opt out by passing `\"none\"` as the retention policy\n- The breakpoint uses the `\"ephemeral\"` type, which is Anthropic's standard for prompt caching\n- This strategy ensures stable, reusable cache across conversation turns while remaining optional"
