      "answer": "Perfect! Now I can provide you with a clear answer:\n\n## Prompt Cache Breakpoint for Claude Requests\n\nThe prompt cache breakpoint for Claude requests is placed in **two strategic locations**:\n\n### 1. **System Message** (`apps/server/providers/src/claude/stream-fn.ts`, line 211)\nThe system message itself gets a cache control marker when `cacheRetention !== \"none\"`:\n\n```typescript\nsystem: retention === \"none\"\n  ? trimmedSystem\n  : [{ type: \"text\", text: trimmedSystem, cache_control: { type: \"ephemeral\" } }]\n```\n\n### 2. **Trailing Conversation Block** (`apps/server/providers/src/claude/convert.ts`, lines 198-206)\nAfter merging all conversation messages, the **last block of content in the final message** gets marked with the cache control breakpoint:\n\n```typescript\n// Trailing prompt-cache breakpoint over the conversation prefix, so the\n// stable history caches across turns. Skipped when the caller opts out.\nif (cacheRetention !== \"none\") {\n  const lastMessage = merged[merged.length - 1]!;\n  const lastBlock = lastMessage.content[lastMessage.content.length - 1];\n  if (lastBlock) {\n    lastBlock.cache_control = { type: \"ephemeral\" };\n  }\n}\n```\n\n**Key points:**\n- Both use `{ type: \"ephemeral\" }` as the cache control type\n- The breakpoint is only added when `cacheRetention !== \"none\"` (respects the caller's cache preference)\n- This strategy allows the stable conversation history to cache across turns while marking the latest content as ephemeral (eligible for new caching on the next turn)"
